Context("Numeric");

$q1 = Compute("0"); #M1 Q5.1
$q2 = Compute("5pi/2"); #M1 Q5.2
$q3 = Compute("4pi(237^3-137^3)"); #M1 Q5.3


##############################################################
#
#  Text
#
#

Context()->texStrings;
BEGIN_TEXT
For the first two parts you may give a decimal answer rounded to two decimal places or an exact value. For the last part you may give an exact value or round to the nearest whole number. $BR

Computing flux of a vector field \( F \) through a surface \( S \) takes a few steps: parametrize, orient the unit normal, and  compute. Here is a partially finished example. $BR $BR

1. You want to calculate the outward flux of \( F(x,y,z) = (y,z,0) \) through the boundary of the unit ball \( B = B_1(0,0,0) \) in \( \mathbb{R}^3 \). Here's what you've done: $BR
\( \bullet \) Parametrized \( \partial B \) with \( H(s,t) = (\cos(s)\sin(t),\sin(s)\sin(t),\cos(t)) \). $BR
\( \bullet \) Computed \( (\partial_1 H \times \partial_2 H)(s,t) = \big(-\cos(s) \sin^2(t), -\sin(s)\sin^2(t), -\cos(t)\sin(t) \big) \). $BR
Finish the calculation. Illustrate your work with a sketch. Use WolframAlpha to evaluate  integrals.

\{$q1->ans_rule\}
$BR $BR

2. You want to calculate the outward flux of \( F(x,y,z) = (x,x+y,3z-2) \) through the boundary of the solid paraboloid \( R = \lbrace (x,y,z) \in \mathbb{R}^3 : x^2+y^2 \leq z \leq 1 \rbrace \). Here's what you've done so far:$BR
\( \bullet \) Parametrized a piece of \( \partial R \) with \( P(s,t)= (s,t,s^2+t^2). \)$BR
\( \bullet \) Computed  \( (\partial_1 P \times \partial_2 P)(s,t) = (1,0,2s) \times (0,1,2t) = (-2s,-2t,1). \) $BR
\( \bullet \) Parametrized a piece of \( \partial R \) with \( G(u,v) = (u,v,1). \)$BR
Finish the calculation to compute the outward flux of \( F \) through \( \partial R \). Illustrate your work with a sketch. Use WolframAlpha to evaluate  integrals.

\{$q2->ans_rule\}
$BR $BR

3. You want to calculate the outward flux of \( F(x,y,z) = (x,y,z) \) through the boundary of the solid hollowed out ball \( \lbrace (x,y,z) \in \mathbb{R}^3 : 137 \leq x^2+y^2+z^2 \leq 237 \rbrace \). Here's what you've done:$BR
\( \bullet \) Parametrized a piece of \( \partial R \) with \( G(s,t) = (237\cos(s)\sin(t),237\sin(s)\sin(t),237\cos(t)). \) $BR
\( \bullet \) Computed \( (\partial_1 G \times \partial_2 G)(s,t) = \big(-237^2 \cos(s) \sin^2(t), -237^2 \sin(s)\sin^2(t), -237^2 \cdot \cos(t)\sin(t) \big). \) $BR
Finish the calculation to compute the outward flux of \( F \) through \( \partial R \). Illustrate your work with a sketch. Use WolframAlpha to evaluate  integrals.

\{$q3->ans_rule\}
END_TEXT
Context()->normalStrings;

